Z||000000||Ironhorse||06-19-2000||09:46 PM||||My friend got a 85 fiero 4 cyc with 5 speed.  His timing gear went toothless on his car.  He read in a book that you gotta drop the engine/tranny/cradle, take out the head, pull out the camshaft, sepreate the camshaft and the timing sprocket.  There's gotta be a way to get this stupid timing sprocket (gear) out of the camshaft without doing all that. DANG WHY CANT A 4 CYC BE SETUP WHERE THEY USE TIMING CHAIN AND 2 SPROCET INSTEAD OF THIS STUPID SETUP.. sorry I feel for this guy's fiero cuz *sniffle* it's A FIERO||||reg||1
Z||000001||Phoenix||06-20-2000||09:15 AM||||Here's the scoop - I use to have a 84 Camaro with an Iron Duke and it spit all the teeth off the gears in the middle of South Dakota. I was told the same thing that the camshaft has to be removed. I found another garage in the town where the mechanic did this:<BR>1. Drilled holes around the hub of the cam gear and then split the gear to remove it.<BR>2. The camshaft had a pilot hole in the end that he tapped threads into.<BR>3. He then used a bolt and thrust washers to pull the gear back onto the cam.<P>He did this in less than a day and we were back on the road. Drove all the way to the west coast, all the way down US1 and back home (Minnesota) with no problems. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||dman||06-20-2000||02:42 PM||||Hi Ironhorse, if you and your friend would like to perform the work yourself, here are two links that provide some useful information:<BR> <A HREF="http://members.xoom.com/fierov6/OSG/timing-gear.html" TARGET=_blank>http://members.xoom.com/fierov6/OSG/timing-gear.html</A> <BR> <A HREF="http://www.tir.com/~jaski/l4-timinggear.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.tir.com/~jaski/l4-timinggear.htm</A> <P>||||reg||1
